# Slot modifiers

The bracket's `|…` run is **pure configuration** (§5.4, D-9): every symbol names
a table in `pendon.toml` and contributes that table's fields to the element.
Pendon ships no modifier of its own, so a symbol with no config contributes
nothing and is not a diagnostic — and neither the entry nor the run needs a
custom component to exist (D-8).

@[two symbols |?!](https://example.com/two)

`?` and `!` both write `rel` and `soo.baz`, so two contributions to one path
become an array in **written** order — never sorted, and never deduplicated:
`rel` is `["nofollow", "noopener"]` and `soo.baz` keeps both `"wax"` entries.

@[class accumulates |?](https://example.com/class)@@anchor{.hero, .external}

`class` is the one field that adds up instead of clashing (§6.4), and a token
the element already carries is not repeated.

@[head wins |?!](https://example.com/head)@@anchor{rel: "ugc"}

A modifier is configuration, the head is authored text: `rel` stays `ugc` and
the losing contribution is dropped with a warning.

@[unknown symbols |xyz](https://example.com/none)

`xyz` configures nothing, so this link carries exactly what its head does.

![An image |?](https://x.test/a.webp)

A block image reads the same run: `?` adds `class`, and `loading` is a prop the
`<img>` has no attribute for, so it lands under the `data-` prefix (§6.3).

Minim [^book "hlm. 45" |?]@@citeX{.hero} esse do ut anim proident.

A citation reads the same run, and a `true` contribution is a **bare flag**
(`compact`), never `compact="compact"` (§6.3). The run is configuration, so it
never writes the reference or the locator: the citation still numbers as usual.

#[mod-head |?]("Modifier heading")

This heading and the one above differ only by the run: the entry defines no
component, only a modifier contribution.
